#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""a7a-voice: voice commands on the Cubie A7A. Say the wake phrase, then a command the owner typed in the panel's
Voice tab (a7a-panel): the command line typed beside it runs, as the owner.

  a7a-voice setup          the speech engine (sherpa-onnx, in a venv of its own) and two English models, downloaded
                           once into ~/.local/share/a7a-voice (about 110 MB)
  a7a-voice on | off       the listener now and at every log-in (the user service a7a-voice.service), or neither
  a7a-voice listen         the listener itself (what the service runs)
  a7a-voice status         what the panel shows, as JSON: set up or not, running, what it did last, the settings
  a7a-voice save           new settings, as JSON on stdin (the panel's Save); a running listener takes them within 1 s
  a7a-voice test [--dry | --panel] "<text>"  the command <text> matches, run (--dry: only said; --panel, the panel's
                           Test button: only said when it presses keys or acts on the window, which would be the panel)
  a7a-voice npu on | off   the wake-phrase spotter's network on the NPU, or on the CPU (the default); "on" checks it
                           on a second of silence first, and a listener that cannot start it uses the CPU's
  a7a-voice scan           commands for the menu's applications ("open …", "close …") and common ones (keys, the
                           window, media), found now, into the settings: what the owner changed of one stays
  a7a-voice app open|close ID   the application of desktop file ID started, or its windows closed (as their ✕)
  a7a-voice shortcut NAME  one of KWin's shortcuts ("Show Desktop"), as if pressed
  a7a-voice key KEYS...    keys pressed where the cursor is (dotool's names: enter, ctrl+l, shift+enter, pagedown)
  a7a-voice type "<text>"  <text> typed where the cursor is
  a7a-voice ask [--to NAME] "<text>"  the Assistant's answer to <text> (or NAME's, an online assistant of the
                           panel's settings), as a notification, and read aloud when answers are (what "hey
                           assistant ..." and "hey NAME ..." run)
  a7a-voice speak          whether the Assistant's answers are read aloud, in which voice, as JSON (the Assistant tab)
  a7a-voice speak on | off answers read aloud from now on, or not; the first "on" downloads the voice (about 130 MB)
  a7a-voice speak voice V  the voice that reads them: F1 to F5 or M1 to M5
  a7a-voice say ["<text>"] <text> (else stdin) read aloud now, in place of whatever is being read
  a7a-voice hush           stops what is being read
  a7a-voice check [speak]  loads both models (the voice) and says how long that took (setup's last step)

How it listens. Two small streaming models, run by sherpa-onnx (Apache-2.0) on the CPU, from PipeWire's default
source (the USB microphone on the owner's board) at 16 kHz:
  - a keyword spotter (sherpa-onnx's zipformer, GigaSpeech, 3.3 M parameters, Apache-2.0) waits for the wake phrases,
    all the time: about 15 % of one little core. A phrase typed in the panel works as it is: the model's own
    sentencepiece model splits it into the word pieces it knows (no training). It gets a fresh stream in every pause:
    a stream that has heard much else without its phrase spots it worse (synthetic voices: 1 in 5 against 4 and 5 in
    5; resetting only its search did not help). Two phrases: the commands' ("hey computer") and the Assistant's ("hey
    assistant"): what follows the second is a question for the panel's Assistant, answered in a notification.
  - a recogniser (Kroko's English community model, a zipformer, CC-BY-SA, kroko.ai) writes down what is said after
    the wake phrase, a sentence at a time (a sentence ends after 0.8 s of silence), starting from 1.5 s before the
    phrase was spotted, so a command said in the same breath is not cut. Nothing is written down before the wake
    phrase. Not sherpa-onnx's own small English recogniser (zipformer-en-20M, LibriSpeech): a fresh stream of that
    one drops the first second or two it hears (sherpa-onnx 2.0.1, board 2026-11-18: "AFTER EARLY NIGHTFALL" lost
    from its own test recording, fed whole or 0.1 s at a time, with leading silence or without), which would cut
    the command; Kroko's wrote the same recording down whole from a fresh stream.
In the sentence that holds the wake phrase, only what comes after it counts: what the pre-roll caught before it is
dropped unread, so a command said just before the phrase (by the TV, by someone else) does not run, and nothing said
before the phrase is printed, kept or shown. The command is the one whose words the rest holds best, when it is
close enough (short words must be nearly the same: "minute" is not "mute"); not one negated ("don't lock the
screen"); within 8 s of the wake phrase. One heard word for word runs as soon as it is heard (unless a longer
command's phrase holds it with more words after it), the rest when the sentence ends. A chime when the wake phrase
is heard; a notification with what ran, or with what was heard when nothing matched.
Commands run in a scope of their own (systemd-run --user --scope), so an application started by voice outlives a
restart of the listener.
Answers read aloud (the Assistant tab's "speak answers", for its own answers and those to "hey assistant"): Supertone's
Supertonic 3 (int8, 44.1 kHz, ten voices, 31 languages; MIT code, OpenRAIL-M model), run by sherpa-onnx on the CPU, a
sentence at a time, the next one made while one plays (board 2026-11-19: 0.45 s of work per second of speech on the
two big cores at 4 steps; the model loads in 1.2 s, about 0.5 GB while it reads). Not Piper, Kitten or Kokoro:
sherpa-onnx 2.0.1's wheels have no espeak-ng front end for them ("no frontend available"); Supertonic reads letters.
A wake phrase said while an answer is read stops it (the microphone hears the speaker too).
The NPU (VIP9000) is not used yet: Radxa's own assistant runs the same kinds of model there (Chinese ones,
docs.radxa.com .../npu-dev/voice-assistant); English ones need converting with Allwinner's ACUITY tools first.
Exit 0: done (test: a command matched); 1: failed or nothing matched, the reason printed; 2: usage."""

